{
  "case_id": "2022-22-138",
  "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Billy Counterman",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Counterman moved to dismiss the charge on First Amendment grounds, arguing that his messages were not \u201ctrue threats\u201d and therefore could not form the basis of a criminal prosecution."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0000-0007",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "True threats subject individuals to \u201cfear of violence\u201d and to the many kinds of \u201cdisruption that fear engenders.\u201d"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0001-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Yet the First Amendment may still demand a subjective mental-state requirement shielding some true threats from liability. The reason relates to what is often called a chilling effect."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0001-0002",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The result is \u201cself-censorship\u201d of speech that could not be proscribed\u2014a \u201ccautious and re- strictive exercise\u201d of First Amendment freedoms."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0001-0000",
      "kind": "holding",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That standard, again, is recklessness. It offers \u201cenough `breathing space' for protected speech,\u201d without sacrifcing too many of the benefts of enforcing laws against true threats."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0002-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First, the courts below did not address whether reckless- ness was suffcient to prosecute true threats and neither of the actual parties have advocated a recklessness standard."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0002-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Colorado disclaimed the idea that recklessness was required, and petitioner asserted, correctly, that recklessness had not been raised under traditional principles of party presenta- tion."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0002-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The briefng on recklessness consists almost entirely of a few pages of an argument in the alternative at the tail end of an amicus brief fled by the United States."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0002-0003",
      "kind": "holding",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In such cases, recklessness is amply suffcient. And I would stop there. There is simply no need to reach out in this stalking case to determine whether anything more than recklessness is needed for punishing true threats generally."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0002-0005",
      "kind": "holding",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The majority explained why a prohibition on cross burning with intent to threaten was constitutional, beginning by de- fning the category of true threats. \u201c `True threats,' \u201d the majority explained \u201cencompass those statements where the speaker means to communicate a serious expression of an intent to commit an act of unlawful violence.\u201d Id., at 359 (emphasis added)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0002-0004",
      "kind": "holding",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In sum, all fve Justices in the Black majority agreed that a true-threats prosecution could not stand under the First Amendment without a suffcient subjective mens rea requirement."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0003-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There is no line of cases or pattern of statutes affrmatively stating that an objective standard is suffcient."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0003-0001",
      "kind": "holding",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "While for threats, the speaker need only be \u201caware that others could regard his statements as threatening violence and delive[r] them anyway.\u201d Ante, at 79 (internal quotation marks omitted)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0003-0002",
      "kind": "holding",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I agree with the Court's conclusion that the First Amend- ment requires a subjective mens rea in true-threats cases, and I also agree that recklessness is amply suffcient for this case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0004-0000",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I join Justice Barrett's dissent in full. I write separately to address the majority's surprising and misplaced reliance on New York Times Co. v. Sullivan, 376 U. S. 254 (1964)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0004-0001",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The nature of a true threat points to an objective test for determining the scope of First Amendment protection: Neither its \u201csocial value\u201d nor its potential for \u201cinjury\u201d depends on the speaker's subjective intent. Chaplinsky, 315 U. S., at 572."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0005-0000",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Black plurality's reasoning can be boiled down to the following insight: When context is ignored, true threats cannot be reliably distinguished from protected speech."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0005-0001",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "For starters, he produces no evidence directly addressing the meaning of the First Amendment\u2014nothing from state ratifying conventions, political commentary, or even early debates about efforts to regulate threats in ways that might threaten speech."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0005-0002",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Even if a subjective test had a historical pedigree, the Court's chosen standard of recklessness certainly does not."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0005-0003",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Nor does our First Amendment precedent buttress the Court's preferred standard."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0005-0004",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the Court's holding affects the civil consequences for true threats just as much as it restricts criminal liability."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0005-0005",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In addition, employers and school administrators often discipline individuals who make true threats."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2022-22-138",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fe6940feec1c23c8acc9787e87ad3d644dfea245c08f792187db7da82f48dd56",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fe6940feec1c-0005-0006",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Nonetheless, the Court concludes that Counterman can prevail on a First Amendment defense."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
